WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.660
- JaidenAnimations is
an absolute rock star

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.120
in YouTube animation world.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.730
We're watching one of the medical videos

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:06.970
that she put up on her channel.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.150
If you want me to watch
more, smash that like button

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.170
get a hundred thousand likes,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.050
we'll do more of these.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:11.883
Let's get to it.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.230
- It's Christmas Eve.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.610
You're getting ready for bed
and everything is prepared

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.420
for the perfect Christmas morning.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.590
Everyone has the standard
things on their Christmas list.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.910
Puppies, Nintendo switches.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.590
- I can relate to this so much

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.070
except it's not Christmas
in Russian culture.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.450
Everything that happens on
Christmas happened on New Year's.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:33.530
Instead of a Christmas tree

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.470
we had a New Year's tree.
Instead of Santa Claus

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.550
we had Grandfather Frost, Ded Moroz

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.300
which is basically Santa Claus.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.730
And the gifts that we
got was like markers.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.850
People in America get dogs
and Nintendo switches.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.690
I got a highlighter and I was hyped.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.780
- But you're suddenly awakened

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.570
by a strange sensation.
Your body is burning,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.850
flaring up, itching everywhere.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.310
The worst itching you've ever
experienced in your life.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.170
- I think it's either chicken pox,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.040
measles if they're not vaccinated.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.530
I mean like a lot of
viruses can cause rashes.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.040
A lot of times we don't even diagnose

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.180
what exactly caused the rash.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.280
We just call it a viral exanthem.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.210
- I've never had hives

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.450
until last December.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.020
- Hives are usually a result
of an immune allergic reaction

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.850
an overreaction to something.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.440
You know, I actually had
an experience with hives

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.440
an unknown, also known as
idiopathic form of hives.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.510
After I came back from
the beach, like I was in

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.650
one of the Brooklyn
beaches, I went swimming.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:24.730
Like the water is so gross.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:25.930
And then I came out, I was fine.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.610
I put sunblock on, next day,
woke up, my whole lower body

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.030
including my butt was covered in hives.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.070
And they kept coming back
and getting worse and worse

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:33.903
like they would just cycle.

00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:36.420
So I ended up having to
take steroids, oral steroids

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.253
in order to get rid of them.

00:01:37.253 --> 00:01:40.050
It was really problematic and
no doctor, including myself

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.210
or my doctor, or my doctor,

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.980
or my dad could figure out why it is.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.140
I had these hives. That happens sometimes.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.550
- Around the night of the 21st.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.130
I was relaxing and playing
video games on my couch.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.350
Ari was on me because it's snugly and nice

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.040
but about an hour or so
in, I started feeling itchy

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.340
around my neck and shoulders
where he'd been sitting.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.310
I didn't think much of it

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.680
but when I was getting ready for bed

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.530
I realized the area that was
itchy was actually kind of red.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.730
- That's actually a reasonable
thought process there.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.320
The thing is with animals,
they sometimes carry specific

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.570
bacterias. Parakeets
have a form of chlamydia

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.570
that they carry that causes pneumonia.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.510
And you basically have to
really know which animal

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.730
that you've been exposed
to in order to rule out

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.990
potential bacterial
infections, like there's

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.050
a popular one that we
use on medical boards

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.470
related to rabbits, and
it's called tularemia.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.910
- 4:00 am, I'm woken up by more itchy.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.910
I itched it and it got double edgy.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.530
- She mentioned earlier
that she has hives.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.680
That can be as a result
of an allergic situation

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.860
but you could also have
itchiness presenting

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.950
at an early form of cellulitis,
which is a superficial skin

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.690
infection, which can be problematic
because it could spread.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.210
You'll start seeing an area of redness

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.280
that gets worse and worse.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.080
You could even get streaking
which is like redness spots

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.440
or splotches higher up or
in different areas spreading

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.410
from that, that can become
quite problematic because then

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.100
it could seed either into
your blood, into your joint

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:54.933
into your bone.

00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:56.690
That could become really scary because

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.860
at that point you might
need IV antibiotics

00:02:58.860 --> 00:02:59.900
for an extended course.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.480
- I went to the bathroom
to see what was up

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.240
and the area that was irritated
before was even more red.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.240
- And also getting any kind of cellulitis

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:07.910
on your neck is obviously problematic.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.920
Cause it's a very tight
space, small space.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.580
So any swelling in this area
can compromise your airway

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.570
and spread to a lot of vital blood vessels

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.030
that end up going into the brain.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.410
And the last thing you want
is an infection in your brain.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.150
But the reason you don't want it is

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.520
because if you form an
abscess in your brain

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.060
which is basically a pocket

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.910
of an infection where
your body tries to wall

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.900
off the infection into a little bubble

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.020
almost like a cyst that
can become problematic

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.810
because if it starts spreading
and becoming bigger, inflamed

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.960
that can put pressure on some vital

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.390
neurological structures there

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.770
essentially causing brain
death if it's severe enough

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:40.810
and in the wrong area.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.690
- The next morning, after
a bad night's sleep,

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.710
I got out of bed and whoop
it spread to my back.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.870
Yup. This is not good.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:50.350
Did I do anything about it? Nope.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.950
I was like, well, I sure hope
this cures itself somehow.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.260
- Don't think rashes will
automatically cure themselves.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:58.340
If you have something going on

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.680
that's spreading, that's worsening.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.840
That's a sign that you should
contact a health professional.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.290
In fact, that's what I told Michael Reeves

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.140
when we had our little chat
about his bladder exploding

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.500
That whole interview is linked down below.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.980
- But after like an hour
of sweating from discomfort

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.320
and getting nothing done, I decided, yeah,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.120
I should probably go to urgent care.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.140
- Have you eaten anything new
or that you're allergic to?

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:19.973
- No.

00:04:19.973 --> 00:04:21.210
- Have you taken any medication

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.340
that's new or you're allergic to?

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.173
- No.

00:04:24.173 --> 00:04:26.730
- Have you been bitten by any
bugs you might be allergic to?

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:27.770
- These are all great questions.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.540
And we have to ask this,
remember the most important part

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.610
of a medical exam is the history.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.510
Knowing what potentially
could have happened

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.720
ruling out conditions,
ruling in conditions

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.700
and then creating a list
of differential diagnoses.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.130
What's most likely what's
least likely, putting the ones

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.990
at the top that are most
deadly so that you can

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.740
rule them out and they can't
cause any true harm right now.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.980
- Hm... It looks like dermatitis.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.010
- I promise doctors don't have
a checklist and they're just

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.060
like crossing things off
that happens mentally.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.470
And by the way, dermatitis is
kind of like a catch-all term

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.310
that basically means
inflammation of the skin.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.820
But you need to know why
you're having dermatitis.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.410
- I ran into my room and
immediately took the medication

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.670
and started hoping I
would feel better soon.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.300
All I could do was lay
on my bed and do nothing

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.430
because every time I
moved or touched something

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.660
it would send shocks of pain
and an irritation down my body.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.550
- I also hope that the
urgent care physician

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.680
mentioned to her that if
you have X happen to you

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.810
you should come back or you
should go to the emergency room.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.110
Because patients need to feel
empowered to know what to do

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.300
in case something got worse.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.660
And here, it sounds
like it's getting worse.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.030
It's getting painful. She can't move.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:27.370
Those are all signs that
this thing is not improving.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:30.340
- I told my friend I'm in
so much pain and discomfort.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:31.460
I can't think straight.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.740
And I've been sitting on the floor

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.610
not being able to move for 30 minutes.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:35.592
What should I do?

00:05:35.592 --> 00:05:36.425
- The ER.

00:05:36.425 --> 00:05:37.320
- Go to the hospital?

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.270
- You think so?

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.710
- Yes. Go to the hospital.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:40.543
- I don't know.

00:05:40.543 --> 00:05:41.620
Maybe if I just wait a bit more-

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:42.500
- Go to the hospital.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.333
- Okay.

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:44.166
- That's a good friend.

00:05:44.166 --> 00:05:45.150
- I arrived at the hospital.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.540
I was finally taken to a bed

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.870
given a shot along with a mystery pill.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.440
And about 40 minutes later, I felt better.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.540
- It sounds like the medication

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.560
that she was given was oral steroids.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.310
And maybe the shot was like Toradol

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.640
which is an anti-inflammatory medication

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.850
and pain reliever as well.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.720
- The hives had been
manageable the whole day

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:03.450
until I was getting ready to go to bed.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.440
And they started flaring up again.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.400
This time worse than ever.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.230
I thought I'd experienced pain before

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.020
but the hives were mocking me.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.880
The previous ones were teasers.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.660
This time they were
dishing up the main course.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.990
Everything was put into mega gear

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.180
served up on the grill
of Gordon Ramsey himself.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.540
My dad decided to take
me to a local urgent care

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.610
because they've got a family
friend who works there.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.720
It was more familiar.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.880
The doctor walked in and was
like, yep, looks like hives.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.320
And stuck me with an EpiPen.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.320
- In this case, I don't
know how the urgent care

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.440
doctor decided to use an EpiPen where she

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.370
didn't talk about anything
of the airway swelling.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.340
Didn't talk about her blood
pressure being really low.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.870
But if it was the case that
her blood pressure was dropping

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.720
the hives were getting
worse, potentially yeah,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.140
that could have been
an anaphylactic attack

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.250
just presenting itself weird.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.370
Also another symptom to
look out for in anaphylaxis

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.230
that a lot of people
don't know is a feeling

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.170
of like abdominal pain
with nausea, vomiting

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:56.680
cause that can sometimes
present itself in that way.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:57.970
Plus upper airway swelling.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.210
So you have to really be attuned

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.670
to listening for those symptoms.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.990
- Anyway, problem solved.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.930
The doctor didn't really
say anything else.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.510
He kind of just gave me the
EpiPen and sent me on my way.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.240
- Actually I'm not happy with
this treatment plan here.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.073
I'll explain why.

00:07:10.073 --> 00:07:12.010
If you give someone an EpiPen,
you have to monitor them

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.320
for an extended period of
time, at least four hours

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.250
or so to make sure that
their vital signs are okay

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.350
because you're giving someone
artificial adrenaline.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.930
Their heart can sometimes go
into an arrhythmia as a result.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.270
So you have to monitor these patients.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.600
Also, another important reason is

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.230
20% of the first EpiPens
fail after a few moments.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.280
You know you give someone
EpiPen, they feel great,

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:33.050
They leave and then like an
hour later, two hours later

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.430
they have a delayed
reaction happening again.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.640
Also, I never prescribe one EpiPen.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.910
I always prescribe two
because what happens?

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:42.350
You keep one at home, but
you need one on the go.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.660
You need to keep one in your pocket

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.810
in your purse, in your
backpack, whatever it is because

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.540
if you're out and you're
allergic to like peanut dust

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:49.890
and someone gives it to you,

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.190
great that you have an EpiPen at home,

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.023
that doesn't help you.

00:07:52.023 --> 00:07:53.960
- I can have a nice Christmas after all

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.240
with my family and live on in peace.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.550
8:00 AM, woke up, hives all over.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:05.210
This time, I wanted to just
close my eyes again and perish.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:06.780
- Surprise, surprise.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.600
- I didn't even know it was possible

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:10.500
to experience this level of itchy

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.440
combined with unbearable pain.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:16.210
The itchiness was so extreme
it felt like needles all over.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:19.080
Every time something just grazed my skin

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:22.120
it felt like, yep, this
is, it's time to die.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.070
- You have a lot of nerves
on your cutaneous level,

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:25.903
meaning on your skin,

00:08:25.903 --> 00:08:27.610
because that's how you
interact with the world.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:28.470
It's the sense of touch.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.250
So anytime those nerves are
inflamed, it's really painful.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.476
And you have basically
hyperesthesia where like

00:08:34.476 --> 00:08:36.890
everything you touch, anything that bumps,

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.650
you hurt so much more than usual.

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:39.940
- Arrived at urgent care.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.220
Couldn't move, looked like
a boiled diseased shrimp.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.550
First of all, I got another EpiPen. Yay.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.350
And then he prescribed me
like four new medications.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.350
None of which I remember the names of,

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:53.220
but one of them was steroids.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.610
- She didn't get steroids before?

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.090
What kind help was she getting?

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:57.780
Ridiculous. And interestingly enough

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.600
we also give sometimes
medications that are

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.200
H2 blockers that we normally
give for stomach acid.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.980
Those work in like a histamine related way

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.060
that also decreases allergic reactions.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:09.780
- The only thing we could conclude was

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.830
it was just caused by
huge buildup of stress.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:14.980
I'd been really busy and
burnt out from a bunch

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.420
of conventions and traveling
up until that point.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:21.190
So they said, yeah, stress
combined with an immune system

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.210
glitch is probably what caused it.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.500
- Essentially what we're saying
though is idiopathic hives

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.160
which is what I mentioned
earlier that I had.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:28.900
Idiopathic means we have no idea.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:29.840
It almost sounds funny cause

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.230
it almost sounds like idiot doctor.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.040
But the reality is that
these things happen

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.160
because we're exposed to
so many little chemicals

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.660
and nutrients in our everyday lives.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.210
Whether it's in the food that we eat,

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:43.280
the skincare products we
use, stress absolutely

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.520
makes it worse and is a factor here,

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.010
but it's not the definitive thing.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.340
Because if we were all stressed

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:51.240
we would all just start
breaking out in massive hives.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.690
And if it does happen again,

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.330
that's how we can start figuring out

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.780
why it's happening to
see what patterns emerge

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.910
from those types of situations.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:01.780
Michael Reeves recently
said his urine went black

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.120
and his bladder popped;
check out my interview

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.920
with him here to figure out what's what.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.748
As always stay happy and healthy.

00:10:07.748 --> 00:10:10.248
(lo-fi outro)

